            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, it was determined that your house is extremely leaky. I recommend that you have some air sealing work done to reduce excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** In your crawl space around all pipe penetrations - especially under your hot water tank. 



** In general, the underside of your floor is allowing significant air leakage into the belly bag of the original trailer. This is likely what is causing your cold floors. 



** There is a heat register and duct in the floor of your work room that does not appear to be connected to anything, and it is pulling cold air into your home. I suggest investigating this further as I was not able to find a vent on the outside of the house for this, nor was I able to get close enough to access it during my inspection. 



** The fresh air duct into your furnace has been disconnected and is not directing fresh air into the furnace. Instead it is pulling fresh air into the furnace closet. I recommend you have a qualified HVAC contractor in to have this reconnected. 



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a 22% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $280 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 8.7 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The above foundation exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 with R12 batt insulation. I recommend you add a minimum R20 insulation to these exterior walls, in conjunction with a future planned upgrade to your exterior siding. I recommend that if you do upgrade your siding, you continue the insulation down over the header between your crawlspace and the main floor of your house for additional savings. 



The new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) that was launched on April 1, 2019 can help you cover the cost of the additional insulation. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added to the above foundation exterior walls ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).



Savings: By adding a minimum R20 to all of the above foundation exterior walls, you could potentially save $900 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 28.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $8,000 in rebates from the DHERP.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



If you are considering completing a siding upgrade and adding exterior insulation prior to March 31st 2022, please contact me.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic space of your home appears to have a total of R20 of insulation, comprised of the original fiberglass batt insulation I have modeled the energy savings if you were to increase the attic insulation up to a minimum R50. Please be sure you maintain adequate attic ventilation and do not block any soffit venting.



Savings: By increasing all of the attic insulation from R20 up to a minimum R50, this is estimated to save $430 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 13.3 GJ, and qualify for a $375 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program insulation rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</ceilings>
                <foundation>

Your crawlspace walls appear to be 2x4 framing with R12 insulation. I have modelled adding R20 rigid foam insulation to the header of your crawl space, in conjunction with an exterior insulation upgrade. 



Savings: By adding 3.5 inches (R14) of batt insulation within the framing, plus 2????????? (R10) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the interior of your crawlspace walls, you could potentially save $ XXX per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by XX GJ, and qualify for up to a $ 1,000 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</foundation>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 83% efficient oil furnace. Due to limitations with oil furnaces, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a 95% AFUE condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $990 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 27.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. In addition, LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Pass



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fan and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 3.7 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace and your wood stove vent up chimneys and are susceptible to back drafting. 



During my assessment of your home, I did see a smoke detector, but no carbon monoxide (CO) detector present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

        - a range hood   

        - one bathroom fan

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan that seals well, uses little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife

The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $1.21 per liter 

Propane - $0.683 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,

Alexandra Giroux, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: alexandra.giroux@aea.nt.ca

Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

